# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Method sources and run artifacts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The methods HarnessGrad adapts are OTHER PEOPLE'S repositories. They are not
# vendored: legacy/adapters/SOURCES.md records what was read and legacy/adapters/fetch.sh
# retrieves it. They live as a sibling directory of this repo, so a clone of
# this repo contains code and records -- not datasets, papers or their licenses.
*_runs/

# Run artifacts: every run contains a workspace that is itself a git repo, so
# committing runs/ would nest repositories and grow without bound.
runs/

# Runs archived rather than deleted, when a batch was declared meaningless. Same
# reason as runs/, and named separately so an archive is never mistaken for a live
# result a reader could compare against.
runs_discarded_*/

# Non-code assets that arrive attached to method repositories. Kept out so a
# clone of this repo contains code and records, not datasets and papers.
# NOTE: lock files are deliberately NOT ignored -- they belong in the repo,
# they are what makes an environment reproducible.
*.pdf
**/docs/assets/
**/predictions/
**/logs/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Credentials
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `.env` holds real keys for the model behind the harness. It is written by hand
# and never committed; `.env.example` is the committed template that says which
# variables a run needs. This rule is added before the file exists, because a
# secret that has been committed once is compromised even after it is deleted.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
# `.env.example` is deliberately NOT ignored: it is the committed template that
# says which variables a run needs. A bare `.env.*` pattern would swallow it, and
# the negation below would not rescue it -- git does not re-include a file whose
# own pattern matched. Enumerating the concrete names is the reliable form.

# Exported harnesses: the finished artifacts of runs, browsable rather than buried
# in a run's git. They are copies -- the commits they came from stay in runs/ --
# so regenerating them is `python tools/export_harnesses.py`.
current_harness/

# Harness working trees. Outside the platform tree on purpose: a harness placed
# inside it can reach the driver and .env through `..`.
harnessgrad_work/

# 服务 PID 文件:本地运行状态,不该进版本库
.harnessgrad-state/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- local notes ---
# Everything under `docs/` is a local working note unless it is named below. The
# pages a user of the platform needs are re-included explicitly.
#
# The default is *hidden* on purpose: a new note is private until someone decides to
# publish it, and a public page cannot be hidden by accident. INTERFACE.md, at the
# repository root, is the contract and is public.
/docs/*
!/docs/resume-design.md
!/docs/writing_a_harness.md
!/docs/dataset-onboarding.md
!/docs/local-model-endpoints.md
!/docs/dataset-first-day.md
!/docs/egress.md
!/docs/moving-docker-data-root.md
!/docs/architecture.md
!/docs/import-direction.md
!/docs/test-suite.md
!/docs/data-split-plan.md
# The split design is a decided methodology the report cites, not a note: it carries the
# measured reuse graph (MuSiQue 2417 -> 544 components) and the sizing rule the study set
# depends on. Tracked by this exception rather than by `git add -f`.
!/docs/eval-split-design.md
# The lane assignment is not a note: `tools/run_tests.py` points at it as the reason
# its EXCLUSIVE tuple looks the way it does, and `tests/quality/test_test_lanes.py`
# fails without it.
!/docs/test-lanes.md
# Registry-linked source reviews are checked-in evidence, not private notes.
!/docs/dataset-reviews/
!/docs/musique-output-contract.md
!/docs/w-fix-unknown-isolation-records.md
!/docs/w-obs-runtime-review.md
!/docs/w-shared-verifier-review.md
# The three below are evidence a reader has to be able to reach without this
# machine: an inventory that Stage D depends on, the discovery-order finding that
# decides whether the split is a pure move, and the split construction for the
# score(N) study. Each is cited by code or by another document, so a clone without
# it has a dangling reference rather than a missing note.
!/docs/tau-bench-vendoring.md
!/docs/data-layout-open-items.md
!/docs/registry-discovery-order.md
!/docs/eval-split-design.md
# A decision the registry no longer records, because the requirement was withdrawn rather
# than met: without this file the removal of `paired-filesystem-quotas` would look like a
# deleted blocker instead of a reviewed decision. Cited from `docs/dataset-blockers.json`.
!/docs/filesystem-quota-decision.md
# The sibling decision to the quota withdrawal, covering the other three requirements
# whose only content was proof that an enforcement mechanism cannot fail. Cited from
# `docs/dataset-blockers.json` and from `tools/check_dataset_blockers.py`.
!/docs/enforcement-proof-decision.md
# The deferral record for the independence and custodian-approval gates: without it the
# withdrawal of those blockers would read as 'not needed' rather than 'operator's to
# review'. Cited from `docs/dataset-blockers.json` and from tests/quality.
!/docs/independent-review-deferred.md
# The review that answers terminal-bench's `cached-runtime-admission`; the registry points
# at it as the evidence behind that entry's `observed`.
!/docs/terminal-bench-runtime-admission.md

# The decision record for withdrawing the last three evidence blockers, and the scope note
# that separates a `LIMIT` withdrawal from a finding of unobtainability. Without it the three
# removals read as "not needed" rather than "withdrawn on purpose, with named limits".
# Cited from `registry/withdrawn-requirements.md`, `registry/blocker-closure-assessment.md`
# and `tests/quality/test_appworld_trusted_trial_writer_codex183.py`.
!/docs/evidence-status-decision.md

# The long-form companion to the front page: the reasoning behind the platform's shape,
# the defects building it caught, and the operating rules a user must not get wrong.
# Without it `README.md`'s "Documentation" table and its rationale pointer both dangle,
# and the detailed design record would exist only in this machine's working tree.
!/docs/design-notes.md

# The specification for the one thing this repository cannot currently produce: the runtime
# observation that `admit_runtime` validates and that `_refuse_unobserved_policies` refuses
# without. It is written to be handed to whoever writes that module, so it has to be readable
# from a clone rather than from this machine's working tree; `docs/runtime-observation-brief.md`
# is cited by the container-dataset refusals it explains.
!/docs/runtime-observation-brief.md

# The handover for onboarding a Harbor dataset: the port-or-wrap decision, the adapter anatomy
# (`metric.py` is the gate-3 artifact, `parity_experiment.json` its equivalence evidence), and the
# delivery-shape rule that decides the `demand` label. It is handed to the dataset side, so it has
# to survive a clone; `docs/dataset-onboarding.md` stays the single source for the five gates and
# this file only records what changes when the dataset comes from Harbor.
!/docs/harbor-dataset-handover.md

# The protocol for producing an H0 -> H1 harness-evolution result: the control arm the corpus
# proves is necessary, the two structural limits of the staged path (`staged_task_set` forces
# `trials=1`, so `ci_available` can never be true; `_require_publication` withholds the score),
# and the rule for what counts as a result. It has to survive a clone because a future run is
# launched from it and because the limits are read *before* a run rather than after a receipt;
# `docs/runtime-observation-brief.md` remains the specification for the one thing that would
# unblock the staged path, and this file does not restate it.
!/docs/harness-evolution-protocol.md

# A `git bundle` of this repository contains **all** of its history, including the
# local notes above if they were ever tracked. Never committable.
*.bundle

# ------------------------------------------------------------ local scratch ---
# Codex/review scratch trees: never source. Keep pytest basetemp outside the platform.
.scratch/
.overleaf-mcp/

# Build output and dataset bytes do not belong in git. Both are reproducible:
# `cd web && pnpm build` rebuilds the SPA, and `tools/datasets.py --fetch --dataset tau_bench`
# plus tools/build_tau_bench_gt.py regenerates the tau-bench databases.
web/dist/
data/datasets/tau_bench/db/

# --- Resource payloads must never enter git -------------------------------
# A live-evidence worker downloaded real packages (conda/whl/tarballs) and saved
# upstream HTTP bodies and multi-MB dumps under tests/fixtures/hg_*/ and reports/.
# That put 516 MB of binaries into the object store for no reason: the EVIDENCE is
# the recomputed digest list, not the payload. Keep the digests and the scripts;
# keep the payloads on disk (they are reproducible from the recorded URLs+hashes).
tests/fixtures/**/*.whl
tests/fixtures/**/*.conda
tests/fixtures/**/*.tar.bz2
tests/fixtures/**/*.tar.gz
tests/fixtures/**/*.tgz
tests/fixtures/**/*.zip
tests/fixtures/**/*.body
tests/fixtures/**/*.strace.txt
tests/fixtures/**/*.incomplete
tests/fixtures/**/*.deb
tests/fixtures/**/*.body.gz
tests/fixtures/**/*.readelf.stdout.txt
reports/**/*.deb
reports/**/*.body.gz
reports/**/*.readelf.stdout.txt
reports/**/*.whl
reports/**/*.conda
reports/**/*.tar.gz
reports/**/*.body

# Per-worker evidence packets (see the note above): kept on disk, never in git.
tests/fixtures/hg_*/

# The 36 exported review documents the active registry cites as `/site/...`
# external references ARE evidence, so they are tracked at their resolver path
# (`tests/quality/conftest.py:24`). They were deleted from the working tree with
# `reports/` and `docs/`; `git show 1825cd0e^:<path>` still carries every one of
# them and `tests/fixtures/hg_migration_site/PROVENANCE.json` records the exact
# source path, commit and digest for each. 1.8 MB of text, no payloads.
!tests/fixtures/hg_migration_site/
!tests/fixtures/hg_migration_site/**
# `docs/experiment_noise.md` is cited by **six** modules -- `data/datasets/alfworld.py`,
# `data/datasets/reasoning_gym.py`, `harnessgrad/records.py`, `harnessgrad/loops/mode_a.py`,
# `harnessgrad/store/schema.py` and a quality test -- and it was never committed, so every
# clone had six dangling references to a note that existed only in one working tree. It is
# the measurement behind "a move this small is noise", which is the rule those modules apply
# to every published number; a reader cannot check that rule without it.
!/docs/experiment_noise.md
